{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. Raymond became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his young age a regency was established led by his mother and later by his uncle Baldwin III of Jerusalem. His minority was a turbulent time as rival factions vied for control of the county. The region faced constant threats from Muslim powers and internal disputes among the nobility.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III matured into a skilled diplomat and military leader. He spent much of his reign navigating the complex politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a close ally of the king but often clashed with the military orders especially the Knights Templar. His relationship with the royal court was strained due to his ambition and his belief that he had a rightful claim to the throne of Jerusalem. He was briefly regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of King Amalric I. During this time he tried to consolidate power but faced opposition from the nobles who preferred Baldwin IV. Raymond was known for his cautious approach to warfare and preferred negotiation over direct conflict. He formed alliances with Muslim leaders including Nur ad-Din and later Saladin to protect his territory.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a key role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against the rash campaign led by Guy of Lusignan. Raymond opposed the decision to march to relieve the siege of Tiberias. His warnings were ignored and he was forced to join the crusader army. After the disastrous defeat at Hattin Raymond was captured by Saladin. He was imprisoned for a short time but eventually released. He returned to Tripoli and died in 1187 shortly after his release. His death marked the end of a long and complex reign that shaped the fate of the County of Tripoli. Raymond was remembered as a shrewd but often misunderstood leader who tried to preserve stability in a fractured land. His legacy is tied to the decline of the Crusader states and the rise of Saladin.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0013335392577573657}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.2305197237292305e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.01815054751932621}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.0251946150674485e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.011605959385633469}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.006394876167178154}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.08049094676971436}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.775113539944869e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009245174005627632}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0002057340752799064}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.992108420585282e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.31327706575393677}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0012085284106433392}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -3.4570634852570947e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.3841574147809297e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"to